From f552ab0a2cb27c7fe7a73bd3993a25b1dd1a15b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:04:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] ci: require the macOS native-memory lane in the v2
 aggregate when a PR touches the Darwin memory reader

The no-subprocess tests for hermes_cli/machine_memory_darwin.py are
platforms("macos") and skip on every Linux lane. Only native-memory-macos.yml
ran them, from its own pull_request trigger, outside "All required checks pass
(v2)": on PR #1055 the aggregate went green 29 minutes before the macOS job
finished, and a mutant that put the sysctl/vm_stat subprocess pair back into
_mem_used_pct passed all Linux CI.

GitHub's needs cannot reach a job in another workflow, so the lane moves under
CI the way hermes-state-macos did:

- classify_changes.py gains a native_memory lane: exactly the workflow's six
  paths (NATIVE_MEMORY_PATHS), plus an empty or capped diff (fail open). A
  generic .github/ change does not start it, so other PRs are unaffected.
- detect re-exports it and masks it on a non-release main push (the workflow's
  own push trigger still runs main, as before).
- ci.yaml calls native-memory-macos.yml (now workflow_call, no pull_request
  trigger) for same-repo PRs, and all-checks-pass needs it.
  required_results.py requires the job whenever detect selects the lane, so a
  skipped (fork), failed, cancelled or unreported run keeps the gate red.

tests/ci/test_native_memory_gate.py replays classifier -> detect action ->
ci.yaml detect -> caller -> the aggregate's real evaluate step: a PR touching
hermes_cli/machine_metrics.py fails the gate unless the native job succeeds,
and a PR touching none of the six paths passes with the job skipped. It fails
on the parent (24 failed) and passes here.
